<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bundela</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bundela Rajputs</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bundelas">Bundelas</a> are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> clan of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> who gave their name to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bundelkhand">Bundelkhand</a>, the region between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a> and the Vindhya hills. Rising to power in the 16th century, they ruled from Orchha, where the Mughal ally Bir Singh Deo built palaces and temples. In the late 17th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhatrasal">Chhatrasal</a> Bundela founded the kingdom of Panna, and Bundela houses ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> such as Orchha, Datia and Panna until 1947.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bundela.jpg" alt="Bundela" width="232" height="494" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mydust · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bandela.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>According to the historian Jaswant Lal Mehta, the name Bundela comes from a goddess, Bind-bhasini Devi, believed to dwell on the Bindhachal, the northernmost part of the Vindhya ranges. The region the clan came to rule, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bundelkhand">Bundelkhand</a>, was known in ancient times as the Chedi Kingdom.</p><p>Bundela legends tell how Sohanpal, the younger son of Arjunpal, ruler of Mahoni, won a kingdom of his own. His elder brother Birpal succeeded their father, and Sohanpal sought help from Naga, also called Hurmat Singh, the Khangar ruler of Kurar (Kundar), who demanded a marriage alliance in return. Sohanpal refused and escaped when Naga tried to detain him. Turned away by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chauhan">Chauhans</a>, the Salingars and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kachhwaha">Kachwahas</a>, he was finally helped by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parmar-rajputs">Panwar</a> chief, Panpal, and their joint army defeated Naga in 1288. Sohanpal killed the Khangar men of the fort, sparing the infants on condition that the Khangars serve the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bundelas">Bundelas</a>, and became king of Kurar.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Over several generations cadet lines of the clan founded states across the region from the 16th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudra-pratap-singh">Rudra Pratap Singh</a>, who reigned from 1501 to 1531 and is said to have descended from Sohanpal, moved his capital from Garh Kundar to Orchha in 1531, and Orchha remained the parent Bundela kingdom. Datia separated from it in 1626, and Panna also broke away. After the death of Panna's founder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhatrasal">Chhatrasal</a> in 1731, the states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajaigarh">Ajaigarh</a>, Bijawar and Charkhari separated from Panna. The rulers of Chhatarpur called their clan Panwar Bundela in their official records. The Bundela chiefs often fought one another, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> often took advantage of it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bundela” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Alipura became a separate chiefship in the mid-18th century under a Parihar Rajput family, during the break-up of <b>Bundela</b> power in Bundelkhand.</blockquote><a href="/state/alipura-state">Alipura State</a></li><li><blockquote>Banka-Pahari was confirmed as a separate holding by the British in the early 19th century, as one of several small estates left from the break-up of <b>Bundela</b> power.</blockquote><a href="/state/banka-pahari-state">Banka-Pahari State</a></li><li><blockquote>Banpur was a Bundela principality in the hills south of Jhansi, held by a branch of the <b>Bundela</b> clan.</blockquote><a href="/state/banpur-state">Banpur State</a></li><li><blockquote>Beri took shape as a separate holding in the mid-18th century as the <b>Bundela</b> kingdoms broke up, and its chief was confirmed by a British sanad in the early 19th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/beri-state">Beri State</a></li><li><blockquote>Bijawar was founded around 1765 by Bir Singh Deo, a <b>Bundela</b> of the family of Chhatrasal, who carved out his own territory as Bundela power fragmented.</blockquote><a href="/state/bijawar-state">Bijawar State</a></li><li><blockquote>Bijna was recognised as a separate holding under British protection in the early 19th century, one of a number of small <b>Bundela</b> estates confirmed after the British gained supremacy in Bundelkhand.</blockquote><a href="/state/bijna-state">Bijna State</a></li></ul></section>
